course: chemistry
lesson: 14
title: Tarnish and how metals change
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Grandma's best silver spoons come out of the drawer dull and almost black, even though nobody has used them. The silver has tarnished: its surface joined with tiny amounts of sulphur gases in the air and made a thin dark layer.
explain: Tarnish is a chemical change on the very surface of a metal. Silver darkens, copper goes dull brown and then green, and bronze statues in the park grow green streaks.
explain: Not every metal changes. Gold almost never reacts with air, so gold leaf on an old picture frame still shines after hundreds of years, while some metals, like sodium, react with air within seconds and have to be kept under oil.
explain: Where hands rub a brass door knob or a statue's nose every day, the thin dull layer is rubbed off, and the metal stays bright. And a clever trick with foil, baking soda and hot water can even turn the black tarnish on silver back into silver.
fact: Silver tarnishes because it reacts with sulphur gases in the air and makes a thin black layer.
fact: Eggs, onions and some rubber give off sulphur gases, so silver egg spoons tarnish very quickly.
fact: In hot water with baking soda, aluminium foil takes the sulphur back from the tarnish and turns it back into silver; the water is hot, so it is a grown-up's job.
fact: Polishing rubs a little silver away each time, while the foil method gives the silver back to the spoon.
fact: Copper slowly reacts with air and goes dull brown; vinegar with a pinch of salt makes a dull coin shiny again.
fact: A cheap ring with copper in it can leave a harmless green mark on skin, because sweat with its salt and acid reacts with the copper; the mark simply washes off.
fact: Places on brass or bronze that hands touch every day stay shiny, because touching rubs off the thin tarnish layer.
fact: Oxygen is a very reactive gas; a cut apple and a cut piece of some metals both change colour in air, in very different ways.
fact: Sodium metal reacts with air within seconds, so laboratories keep it under oil.
fact: Bronze is copper mixed with tin, and like copper it grows a green patina that protects the metal underneath.
fact: Gold almost never reacts with oxygen or sulphur, so it does not tarnish and is used on domes and picture frames.
fact: Cola has a weak acid that slowly loosens rust on a nail; the story that cola dissolves a tooth overnight is a myth, though sugary acid drinks do harm teeth over time.
fact: Carbon steel knives (not stainless) get dark spots when left wet with acid such as vinegar, so chefs dry them at once.
exercise: Why do silver spoons go dark in a drawer? | Silver reacts with sulphur gases in the air | Silver soaks up the dark dust from the bottom of the drawer | Silver rusts just like iron does | The light in the room fades the silver
exercise: Why does a silver egg spoon tarnish so fast? | Eggs give off sulphur gases | Eggs are far too hot for silver | Egg yolk is a very strong acid | Eggshells scratch the silver
exercise: In the foil and baking-soda trick, what happens to the tarnish? | It is turned back into silver | It is scrubbed off with the foil | It melts away in the hot water | It is painted over with aluminium
exercise: Why is the foil method kinder to old silver than hard polishing? | Polishing rubs silver away, the foil gives it back | Polishing makes the silver go green and crumbly at the edges | The foil adds a gold coat to the spoon | Polishing only works on new silver
exercise: What can make a dull brown copper coin shiny again? | Vinegar with a pinch of salt | A glass of cold milk with sugar | Leaving it out in the sun for a day | Wrapping it in paper
exercise: Why can a cheap ring leave a green mark on your finger? | Sweat reacts with copper in the ring | The ring is covered in green paint | The ring is pulling iron out of your blood | The skin is allergic to all metals
exercise: Is the green mark from a cheap ring dangerous? | No, it washes off and is harmless | Yes, it is a sure sign of poisoning | Yes, the skin under it is badly burnt | No, but it will never wash off again
exercise: Why do brass door knobs stay bright where people touch them? | Hands rub off the thin tarnish every day | Hands leave a thin layer of gold on them | Sweat polishes them into new brass every time they are touched | Touched metal never reacts with air
exercise: What is bronze made from? | Copper mixed with tin | Iron mixed with some zinc | Gold mixed with silver | Copper mixed with gold
exercise: Is the green on an old bronze statue mostly damage or protection? | Protection for the metal underneath | Damage that will soon eat the whole statue away | Moss growing on the surface | Paint added by the park keepers
exercise: Why is gold used on church domes and old frames? | It does not tarnish in air | It is the hardest metal there is | It is much cheaper than paint | It turns green in a pleasant way
exercise: Why is sodium metal kept under oil in a lab? | It reacts with air within seconds | It is far too heavy to keep in a glass jar | Oil makes it shine for visitors | It melts at room temperature
exercise: What does a week in cola do to a rusty nail? | Its weak acid loosens the surface rust | It slowly turns the iron nail into sugar | It makes the nail rust even more | It dissolves the whole nail away
exercise: Why do some chefs dry their knives straight after use? | Carbon steel stains when left wet with acid | Wet knives become blunt in just a few minutes | Water makes knives too heavy to lift | Dry knives cut through bones better
exercise: What do a cut apple and a cut piece of reactive metal have in common? | Both change colour by reacting with oxygen | Both turn into orange rust in the air | Both are full of sulphur gases | Both are protected by chromium
test: "my mate says cola dissolves a tooth overnight, legit?" | No, that's a myth, but sugary acid drinks harm teeth over time | Yes, it's totally gone by morning, there's nothing left in the glass | Yes, but only diet cola does it | No, cola has no acid in it at all
test: Grandma's silver teapot is dark; Mia wants to scrub hard with polish every week. What's the catch? | Each polish rubs a little silver away | Polish turns silver into copper | Scrubbing makes the tarnish come back green | Polish makes the silver rust like iron
test: A statue of a dog in the park has a shiny nose but dark ears. Why? | People rub its nose for luck, wiping off the tarnish | Its nose was made of real gold from the very start | Rain falls only on its ears | Dogs in the park lick the ears dark
test: Jake: "gold's dead old on that frame but it's still shiny, someone must polish it". What's the truth? | Gold doesn't tarnish, so it stays bright | Gold is polished every week by museums | The frame is plastic painted gold-coloured | Old gold always goes black eventually
test: Your sister's new ring turns her finger green. She panics: "am I poisoned??" | No, it's copper reacting with sweat, harmless | Yes, go to hospital straight away | Yes, green skin means blood poisoning | No, it means she ate too many green vegetables
test: If you stored silver forks in a box with rubber bands and boiled eggs, what would happen? | They would tarnish faster from the sulphur gases | They would stay shiny forever | They would slowly turn green like an old statue in a park | They would start rusting orange
test: In a cartoon, a scientist leaves a lump of sodium on the desk and goes for lunch. What would really happen? | It would react with the air within seconds | Nothing at all, sodium is as calm as gold | It would slowly go green over years | It would just collect a bit of dust
test: "dropped vinegar on me dad's fancy knife and it got dark spots. is it ruined?" What's going on? | Acid reacted with carbon steel; dry knives fast | The vinegar made the knife magnetic | The steel is slowly turning into silver | The knife is rusting from the inside out
test: Which object would you expect to look the same after a hundred years in a church? | A gold-leaf picture frame | A bright new copper church roof | A silver candlestick on the altar | A big bronze bell in the tower
test: Teacher: "a cut apple and a cut bit of metal both change in air". What makes both happen? | Oxygen in the air reacting with them | Light from the windows bleaching them | Germs from the air eating them | Water from the air freezing on them
exam: Identify the principal cause of the darkening of silver tableware in ordinary domestic conditions. | Reaction with sulphur-containing gases in the air | Absorption of moisture from the atmosphere | Exposure to excessive quantities of light | Contact with oxygen alone in dry air, as in the rusting of iron
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the chemical behaviour of gold in air? | It undergoes almost no reaction and does not tarnish | It reacts rapidly and must be stored under oil | It acquires a green protective patina over time | It is converted into rust within a few years
exam: It is given that a ring containing copper produces a green mark upon the skin of its wearer. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The mark results from sweat reacting with the copper and is harmless | The mark denotes poisoning of the blood and requires urgent medical treatment | The mark results from the ring absorbing iron from the blood | The mark is permanent and cannot be washed away
exam: Determine which of the following assertions concerning cola is consistent with the established facts. | It does not dissolve a tooth overnight, yet sugary acidic drinks harm teeth over time | It dissolves a human tooth completely within a single night | It contains no acid whatsoever and is harmless to teeth | It strengthens tooth enamel when consumed daily
